e early  stages of the u.s invasion so there is a  history of civil strife however the  possibility might be a bit lower than in  the early stages of the u.s invasion  there are more platforms of dialogue  there are more political parties and  political individuals that are very  powerful in today's iraq that would  rather continue or take this forward in  a peaceful dialogue because iraq is  going through since october 2019 protest  movement the political system or the  political class of post 2003 iraq is  going through a very drastic  transitionary period where it is  determined whether this political system  will survive whether this ethnic ethnic  sectarian political system will survive  it has been questioned by the rising  political consciousness of the youth of  the civil society the international  community is also realizing the alarming  effects of the failures of this  political class towards education public  goods public services and the  sovereignty of the iraqi borders  including  the the the the the alarming  impacts on iraqi sovereignty from  equally from different regional power  players including iran  so  this is certainly an issue that will be  considered in the long term however  there will be more platforms of dialogue  that could ensure that this crisis is  more political than it really relates to  the daily lives of wood and iraqi  citizens sure the suggest movement does  by the hearts and minds of so many  people across the spectrum when it comes  to nationalism and reform but in reality  as we noticed in the lowest voter  turnout since uh the the establishment  of the current iraqi democratic system  it proves that most iraqis do not  affiliate themselves with these  political parties and they do not  affiliate with themselves with this  political system and they hope to see a  different political system as they do  it's interesting you're bringing up  these mass protests that happened in  2019 i was in baghdad covered  covered many of those protests in  october and november of that year in  2019  and and i encountered so many members of  these youth protesters who said they had  no faith in the government whatsoever  they wanted a complete overhaul of the  political system i'm curious to get your  perspective if you think that there is a  chance that we might see mass protests  again  like what we saw in 2019  there is  a very big possibility that people  witnessed uh similar mass protests  however unfortunately the the  the focus or the the momentum of the  protest movement has lowered  due to a lot of distractions that  dragged away the attention from the  protest movement and it also created a  lot of  pessimism amongst the iraqi youth  as you probably recall uh right after  the the like shortly after the the the  breakout of the october 2019 protest  movement we witnessed a lot of uh  destabilizing events that took place  whether it was uh the assassination of  basum suleimani whether it was the  political violence attempts committed by  the armed wings of the political parties  within the iraqi government towards the  peaceful protesters  or the political failures and ensuring  to create a caretaker government that  would be different from the similar  faces or from the similar political  parties and interest groups that the  people  initially revolted against from the  first place so this might be just  another event or incident or another  series of incidents that will distract  the people uh pull the people away from  giving them hope that the change is  possible by only protesting however  currently it's in the it's in the it's  the ball is in the court of the  political parties that are trying in one  way or another to save this political  system however if you have players such  as  who would like to redefine the political  system under his own terms for  objectives that he had planned a very  long time ago mohammed what steps do you  think we'll see iran take if if what is  happening now in iraq escalates and if  it causes serious instability  well i think this is happening at a very  sensitive time for iran i don't think  iran wants this unrest to continue iran  is currently engaged in very sensitive  negotiations on its nuclear issue with  the united states it's also engaged in  negotiations with saudi arabia where  iraq is actually hosting these  negotiations so i think these unrest are  actually happening at a very bad time  for iran and i think iran wants these  unrest to end as soon as possible and  nevertheless i don't think iran  has the necessary influence and the  necessary tools to end this  uh i think uh iran will try to negotiate  and facilitate negotiation between the  two sides but as i said before i think  the uh relationship between iran and  murtada al-sadr has deteriorated  in the past year uh gawani which is the  new  head of the puts force of iran  uh he doesn't enjoy the same kind of  relationship that sulaimani enjoyed with  mukhta al-sadr so i think ultimately  iran has to  engage with some sort of negotiation  with mukhta al-sadr to fix the situation  uh marcin um briefly i want to ask you  about  how bad is the situation currently for  ordinary iraqi citizens i mean we're  talking about a political stalemate  we're talking about a political crisis  but how is all this impacting  citizens in a country where there is  such high unemployment where they have  not been able to approve a budget where  there's joblessness where people are so  frustrated how bad are things getting  for normal everyday average iraqis  the last 10 months have honestly been  excruciating for the average iraqi  because of the lack of government and as  you said the lack of a budget there  hasn't really been any room to  implement any improvements in terms of  public services or to be able to  provide for employment through private  sector and sadly through public sector  as well which is unadvisable but iraqis  do look towards that um so  everyday citizens are quite fed up with  the impasse it has been the longest  impasse in iraq's close 2003 history so  it is making life really difficult for  everyone particularly as the climate  crisis has hit iraq really hard with  continuous sandstorms um occurring on a  several times a month that have made  everyday life very very difficult um  when it comes to this actual protest  movement and its effect on everyday life  to be honest its effect on traffic and  on the streets in baghdad hasn't been  very noticeable um there's been less  people outside of their homes than usual  but the city  the high alert in the city is really  is really contained to the areas around  the international zone  all right well we have run out of time  so we're going to have to leave our  conversation there thanks so much to all  of our guests zaidun al-kinani marcina  mari and mohammed musavi  and thank you two for watching you can  see the program again anytime by  visiting our website aljazeera.com and  for further discussion go to our  facebook page that's facebook.com  forward slash aj inside story you can  also join the conversation on twitter  our handle is at aj inside story for me  muhammadan zoom in the whole team here  bye for now  you
 

